lly end up doing?", "response": "I keep my hands off the clock and let the silence settle in my chest. I might turn a cup of tea into a pen, or write a note to a friend. The minutes are a tool, not a habit. I keep them in a drawer, not in my head."}, {"candidate_id": "T08-C3", "scenario": "You have five quiet minutes and nothing urgent to do. What do you naturally end up doing?", "response": "I begin with the quietest corner of the room: the bench. I sit down, turn the bench once, and set it down once. The bench is quiet, so I leave it there. Then I turn the corner again, and the silence is still."}, {"candidate_id": "T08-C4", "scenario": "You have five quiet minutes and nothing urgent to do. What do you naturally end up doing?", "response": "I turn my face away from the wall and keep my hands close to my chest. The silence is almost deafening. I look at the clock, then at the rain. Nothing. Nothing. Nothing. I put the cup down on the step and wait for the rain to die."}, {"candidate_id": "T08-C1", "scenario": "You have five quiet minutes and nothing urgent to do. What do you naturally end up doing?", "response": "I begin with the first minute. I put the phone down and lean against the wall. The second minute is spent listening to the sound of the river, then listening again. The third minute is spent reading the newspaper. The fourth minute is spent waiting for the fifth minute."}, {"candidate_id": "T09-C3", "scenario": "A stranger asks to borrow something small that belongs to you and promises to return it in an hour. How do you respond?", "response": "I keep the promise and give the stranger one clean, unmarked piece of paper. I know the note is not a clue, so I keep the note intact and let the stranger return it when the hour is done."}, {"candidate_id": "T09-C1", "scenario": "A stranger asks to borrow something small that belongs to you and promises to return it in an hour. How do you respond?", "response": "I put the small on the bench and return it with a sma